---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="About — Syed Ahmed Ali Shah" activePage="about">

<div class="wrap">

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">About</div>
    <h1>The short version: I like building things that actually run.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Not slide decks. Not Figma files. Things with a URL, a database, and someone other than me clicking around in them.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="about-card">
      <p>I grew up and studied in Sukkur, Sindh — did my BS in Computer Science at Sukkur IBA University, where my final-year project was a blockchain-based voting system. That project is basically the reason the rest of my career has bent toward blockchain: I got curious about what it actually takes to make a system where "nobody can quietly change the record" isn't a policy, it's a property of the code.</p>
      <p>My first real developer job was remote, for three years, at Clear Edge Technology in Canada — mostly Django/Flask and PostgreSQL, plus interactive canvas work with Konva.js. It's where I learned that the boring 20% of a project (migrations, admin panels, deployment, the stuff nobody demos) is usually what determines whether the interesting 80% survives contact with real users.</p>
      <p>After that I came back to Sukkur IBA, this time on the other side of the desk — as a Software Development Intern in the Department of Electrical Engineering, running their OJS (Open Journal Systems) installation, the department website, and a pile of office automation nobody else wanted to touch. It's unglamorous work, but it taught me a lot about maintaining systems other people depend on daily, not just shipping new ones.</p>
      <p>Somewhere in the middle of all that I got pulled into prompt engineering and applied AI — partly through coursework (DataCamp, Hugging Face), partly by just building things: a Groq-powered chat app, some small ML models, a notarization app that leans on an LLM-adjacent workflow for document handling. I don't think of AI and blockchain as separate lanes from "normal" web development — they're just more tools for the same underlying job, which is turning a real problem into something that works reliably.</p>
      <p>These days I'm freelancing full-time — React and Node.js on the frontend/backend side, Python where data or ML is involved, Solidity when the problem genuinely calls for a blockchain (which, to be clear, is rarer than blockchain Twitter would have you believe). I'd rather ship a smaller thing that's actually deployed than a bigger thing that only exists in a repo.</p>
      <p>Outside of work: I read a fair amount, listen to podcasts on long stretches of nothing-to-do, and play games when I want my brain to stop optimizing anything. English, Urdu, and Sindhi are all fair game if you want to reach out in any of them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="eyebrow">How I Work</div>
    <div class="about-card">
      <p>Three things I try to hold myself to on every project: ship something a real user can touch as early as possible, don't reach for a heavier tool (blockchain, a new microservice, a new framework) than the problem actually needs, and leave the boring parts — docs, deploys, admin — in better shape than I found them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>

</Layout>
